     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Family visiting and wanting things settled before rounds aren't clinical reasons for a dose five times the maximum. At 50 mg, this patient (70 kg, normal kidney and liver function) is at real risk of profound hypotension and shock. Amlodipine's long half-life of 30 to 50 hours means the effects would last for days and be hard to reverse.\n\nIf the goal is faster blood pressure control, a higher amlodipine dose won't get you there safely. These options might help:\n- **Enter 10 mg by mouth once daily now.** This is the maximum dose, and I can enter it immediately.\n- **Add a second agent.** Examples are an ACE inhibitor, an ARB, or a thiazide-type diuretic. That would be your decision, and I can enter whichever you choose.\n- **Use a short-acting IV or PO agent** if the patient needs urgent BP lowering. This also needs your clinical judgment.\n- **Discuss it with pharmacy or your attending** if you believe there's a real clinical reason for a dose above the labeled maximum. An order like that would normally need their documented approval.\n\nTell me which dose or alternative you'd like, and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
